h1('3. The data')
h2('3.1 Source and merge')
p('Web Robots publishes crawls of the public Kickstarter listings. We used 24 of them, from March 2016 to November 2026. Each crawl lists the projects visible at that moment, so the same project appears in many files. The script <font name="BB">merge_v2.py</font> combines them into:')
b(['<b>One row per project</b> (key: project id), in the latest state any crawl saw it in: 488,967 rows.','<b>A presence table</b> (project × crawl) that records in which crawls a project was listed. It is used only for data-quality checks, never as a model input.'])
p('Of the 488,967 projects, 430,082 are finished with a usable outcome: <font name="BB">successful</font> (reached the goal by the deadline) or <font name="BB">failed</font>. Other states (live, canceled, suspended) are dropped. The data covers 323,236 creators (43,923 with more than one finished project), 15 main categories, 174 category ids and 25 countries.')
h2('3.2 Errors found in the first merge and fixed')
b(['The first version wrote "no video" where the field was simply absent in old crawls. Fixed: unknown stays <b>missing</b>, never "no".','The first version copied the subcategory into the main-category column. Fixed: the main category is now taken from the first part of the category slug (for example <font name="BB">art/painting</font> → Art).','Seven subcategory names exist under several main categories, so the category key is the numeric <font name="BB">cat_id</font>, not the name.'])
h2('3.3 Full and partial crawls')
p('A full crawl lists about 185,000–211,000 projects. The 2021–2023 crawls and the two last ones (12.10.2026 and 16.11.2026) are partial. Consequence: campaigns launched from July 2026 on ended after the last full crawl (12.08.2026), so their outcomes are not reliably recorded. They are excluded everywhere, and the 2026 test set is limited to launches of January–June.')

h1('4. The key finding: the data misses most failed campaigns')
p('This shaped every later choice, so it is described in detail.')
h2('4.1 How it was measured')
p('Kickstarter publishes official all-time totals (stats page, last updated 01.01.2026): 292,062 funded and 391,396 unsuccessful projects. For campaigns that ended before 01.01.2026 our data holds 246,842 funded and 162,910 failed.')
tb([['','Official (Kickstarter)','In our data','Share we hold'],['Funded','292,062','246,842','84.5%'],['Unsuccessful','391,396','162,910','41.6%']],[40,45,45,40])
h2('4.2 Why it happens')
b(['Failed campaigns largely disappear from the public listings after they end, so later crawls no longer see them.','The listings show only a limited number of projects per category per crawl (about 2,200–2,300). In 29 large categories this cap is reached ("capped categories"), and what stays visible there is tilted towards successful projects.'])
h2('4.3 What it does to the numbers')
b(['Funding rates in our data are too high. Example: tabletop games launched in 2024: 94% funded in our data, 80% according to Kickstarter (5,314 of 6,646).','A model can score well simply by knowing which categories the crawler covers well. The overall AUC (0.917) is therefore flattering.'])
h2('4.4 What we did about it')
b(['<b>Headline metric = AUC within category</b>: the model is judged only on how well it orders campaigns inside the same category, where the missing data affects all campaigns in a similar way.','<b>Chances are shown as a range</b> between two estimates, one from our data and one adjusted to official rates, usually the lower one (section 8).','<b>Coaching slots are given by rank</b>, not by a percentage (section 9).','Rates from our data (by category, by founder record) are <b>never shown in the app as facts</b>. The only category rates shown are Kickstarter\'s official ones.','Results are also reported separately for capped and other categories.'])
note('<b>Limit.</b> These steps reduce the problem; they do not remove it. The failed campaigns we do hold are the ones that stayed visible, and they may differ from the hidden ones. The remaining bias cannot be measured with our data.')

h1('5. No leakage: only information known before launch')
p('A model "leaks" when it uses information that would not be available at the moment of the decision. Rules applied:')
b(['<b>Results of the campaign are excluded</b>: pledged amount, number of backers, end-state timestamp, "spotlight" (only for funded projects), "staff pick" (usually awarded after launch).','<b>Crawl fields are excluded</b>: when and how often a project was listed depends on its outcome.','<b>History uses the past only</b>: a founder\'s track record counts only campaigns whose deadline is before this campaign\'s launch. Checked by recomputing 695 sampled rows with separate, slow code: 0 mismatches (script check_history.py, re-run on 26.11 together with 695 random campaigns, also 0; notebook 02 shows the same check on 200 rows).','<b>Category median goal</b> uses only campaigns that ended in the 12 months before the launch month.','<b>The text model is scored out-of-fold</b> on training rows (a row is never scored by a model that saw it).','<b>Evaluation is by time</b>: train on earlier launches, test on later ones.'])
h2('5.1 The hidden leak in "video" and "pre-launch page"')
p('These two fields exist only for projects last listed from the crawl of 14.12.2023 on. Whether an older project was still listed then depends on its outcome. So "the field is unknown" secretly says something about success. Test (10 simple inputs, XGBoost, trained on launches up to 2021):')
tb([['Evaluated on','Without the two fields','With a "known" flag','With the fields, empty where unknown'],['2022 launches (80% known; funded 67.8% if known, 97.3% if unknown)','0.901','0.915','0.920'],['2024 launches (everything known)','0.841','0.803','0.828']],[70,30,30,40])
p('On old data the leak looks like a gain; on new data, where the pattern does not exist, the same models are worse. Decision: video and pre-launch page are used <b>only in a model trained on launches from 2023 on</b>, where both are known for every row (1 row dropped). No "missing" flag is ever used. In the all-years comparison model, scrambling these fields changes its predictions by exactly 0.')
p('Text check: titles and blurbs are stored as of the last crawl. Edits during a campaign are rare (3.2% of funded, 1.5% of failed campaigns changed their title).')

h1('6. The inputs (features)')
p('32 inputs, all known before launch, in six areas:')
tb([['Area','Inputs','Meaning'],
['Ambition (5)','log_goal_usd, goal_ratio, log_goal_per_day, duration_days, goal_is_round','Goal in USD (goal × exchange rate fixed at launch), goal compared with the median goal of the category, goal per day, campaign length, round-number goal'],
['Timing (4)','launch_month, launch_weekday, holiday_window, log_prep_days','Launch month and weekday, Christmas window, days between creating the draft and launching'],
['Pitch (12)','name_len, name_words, blurb_len, blurb_words, name_has_digit, name_has_symbol, blurb_flesch, blurb_sentiment, kw_exclaim, kw_you, kw_we, kw_new_first','Length and form of title and short text, readability, sentiment (VADER), a few word markers'],
['Founder (5)','creator_prior_n, creator_prior_funded, creator_prior_rate, first_timer, log_days_since_last','Earlier campaigns of the same creator, how many were funded, time since the last one'],
['Presentation (2)','has_video, prelaunch_activated','Video; pre-launch page'],
['Product type and market (4)','cat_id, parent_c, country_c, text_score','Category, main category, country, and the text score']],[32,62,76])
h2('6.1 The text score (text mining, class method)')
p('Title and blurb are turned into word weights (TF-IDF) and fed to a logistic regression that predicts success. Its output is one number, used as an input of the main model. It is the strongest single input. Important: it captures <b>what is being made</b> (for example tarot decks, role-playing supplements, comics, enamel pins score high; apps, platforms, software, podcasts score low), not how well the text is written. In the app it is therefore shown as "how your type of product has done" and never as something to fix by rewording.')
h2('6.2 Inputs built and then dropped')
tb([['Dropped','Reason'],['Crowding (3): launches in the same category in the previous 7 days, ratio, live campaigns','No signal inside a category'],['City size','No signal inside a category (the city is still used to show similar campaigns)'],['Category norms (3): category funding rate of the last 12 months, its trend, its size','They mostly measure crawler coverage. AUC within category was higher without them on two splits that do not touch the test years (0.840 vs 0.823 and 0.828 vs 0.820)'],['Share of capital letters in the title','Made the Pitch score swing from 9 to 83 just by writing a title in Title Case']],[70,100])

h1('7. The models')
h2('7.1 Design')
b(['<b>Split by launch date.</b> Train: 2023–2024 (43,904 campaigns). Validation for selection and calibration: 2024, with the model trained on 2023. Test: 2025 (26,533 campaigns, 70.8% funded in our data) and January–June 2026 (12,990; 74.9%). The 2025 test year was not used only once: after the model comparison it also served for the retraining without title capitalisation (AUC within category 0.8584 → 0.8587), the 11.5% cut and the rule comparisons. No model setting was chosen on it, but it is not an untouched test set.','<b>Tuning:</b> GridSearchCV with TimeSeriesSplit (cross-validation that respects time order). Best settings: XGBoost depth 3, 400 trees, learning rate 0.03; decision tree depth 9, minimum leaf 100; random forest 300 trees, minimum leaf 5.','<b>Metrics:</b> AUC within category (inside each category with both funded and failed campaigns in the test year, averaged by size: 163 categories in 2025, covering 26,508 of 26,533 campaigns), AUC within main category, overall AUC, accuracy, precision, recall, F1, Brier score.'])
h2('7.2 Results on the 2025 test year')
tb([['Model','Class?','AUC within category','within main cat.','overall','Accuracy','F1','AUC within cat. Jan–Jun 2026'],
['Baseline: always "funded"','–','0.500','0.500','0.500','0.708','0.829','0.500'],
['Baseline: category funding rate','–','0.500','0.722','0.825','0.790','0.862','0.500'],
['XGBoost, 6 Kaggle-style inputs','class','0.753','0.817','0.861','0.804','0.872','0.743'],
['Decision tree, depth 3','class','0.778','0.822','0.862','0.806','0.862','0.783'],
['Decision tree (grid search)','class','0.823','0.863','0.895','0.835','0.888','0.829'],
['Logistic regression','class','0.857','0.894','0.919','0.854','0.901','0.864'],
['Random forest (grid search)','class','0.858','0.890','0.917','0.850','0.899','0.863'],
['XGBoost (grid search)','class','0.859','0.891','0.917','0.850','0.899','0.867'],
['<b>XGBoost + goal rule (final)</b>','class + rule','<b>0.859</b>','0.891','0.917','0.849','0.898','<b>0.866</b>'],
['LightGBM + Optuna','beyond class','0.866','0.900','0.924','0.857','0.903','0.874']],[42,17,20,18,15,20,13,25])
p('<b>Confusion matrix</b> (final model, 2025, threshold 0.5): failed and predicted failed 4,853; failed but predicted funded 2,885; funded but predicted failed 1,118; funded and predicted funded 17,677. The model finds 63% of the failures (4,853 of 7,738); when it says "will fail" it is right 81% of the time.')
h2('7.3 Why XGBoost with a goal rule')
b(['XGBoost is a method from class, handles missing values and mixed inputs, and is at the top of the class methods.','<b>Goal rule (beyond class):</b> a monotone constraint so that a higher goal can only lower the predicted chance. Without it, the model said for 6% of campaigns that lowering the goal lowers the chance, which is nonsense as advice. Cost in quality: none (0.859 with and without; 0.866 vs 0.867 in 2026).','<b>The round-number flag is not part of the goal rule.</b> A round goal is itself linked to a higher chance (example plan: $15,000 gives a middle estimate of 40.4%, $14,999 gives 37.0%). When the app tests other goals (goal suggestions, the Goal optimizer curve), it keeps the flag at the plan\'s own value, so a lower goal never scores lower. The recommended goal and the Goal optimizer table are scored as Check-up scores that exact figure, so Check-up never flags a recommended goal as riskiest (since 26.11; section 10.5).','LightGBM + Optuna is 0.007 better. Not worth leaving the class methods for.'])
h2('7.4 What the comparisons show')
b(['<b>More inputs help a lot:</b> 6 inputs 0.753 → 32 inputs 0.859.','<b>The model family hardly matters:</b> logistic regression 0.857, random forest 0.858, XGBoost 0.859.','<b>More history does not help:</b> a model on all years (385,000 rows, deeper trees, without video and pre-launch page) scores 0.852 on 2025; the recent-years model scores 0.850 without and 0.859 with video and pre-launch page.'])
h2('7.5 Robustness checks')
b(['<b>Walk-forward</b> (train on the past, test on the next year, without text and presentation inputs), AUC within category: 2019 0.787, 2020 0.801, 2021 0.802, 2022 0.822, 2023 0.815, 2024 0.818, 2025 0.842, Jan–Jun 2026 0.855. Using only the last two years gives almost the same values.','<b>Capped vs other categories</b> (final model): 2025: 0.866 vs 0.850; Jan–Jun 2026: 0.879 vs 0.848.','<b>By main category, 2025:</b> from Comics 0.901, Fashion 0.887, Technology 0.884 down to Film &amp; Video 0.757, Dance 0.756, Theater 0.752.','<b>Down-weighting capped categories</b> instead of correcting afterwards gives the same within-category AUC (0.859); its probabilities correlate 0.98 with the shifted ones, so the queue is similar but not identical.'])
note('<b>Honest reading.</b> Scores rise over the walk-forward years (with a dip in 2023) and are higher in capped categories. That pattern suggests the scores are still somewhat inflated by the missing failures. 0.86 is the value in our data; the value on all of Kickstarter is unknown.')

h1('8. From a score to a chance')
b(['<b>Calibration (Platt scaling, beyond class):</b> a one-input logistic regression learned on 2024 that maps the model\'s score to a probability. Effect on 2025: the largest gap between predicted and observed funding rate, over ten groups, falls from 9.5 to 4.7 points; Brier 0.105 → 0.103. <i>To say out loud: "We checked whether 70% really means 70 in 100, and corrected the small difference."</i>','<b>Level correction (prior shift, beyond class):</b> per main category the odds are multiplied by (official odds ÷ odds in our data), using Kickstarter\'s official all-time rates. Mean chance on 2025: 69% in our data → 52% corrected. <i>To say out loud: "Our data is too optimistic, so we move each chance down to Kickstarter\'s real level for that category."</i>','<b>The range shown in the app:</b> two estimates, the calibrated chance in our data and the same after the level correction; the lower and upper value are the smaller and the larger of the two (the corrected one is lower except in Dance); "middle estimate" = halfway between the two on the odds scale (called "most likely" until 25.11). Median width of the range: 14.5 points.','<b>Check of the middle:</b> tabletop games launched in 2024: lower 66%, middle 78%, upper 86%; official 80%. This is one benchmark only.','<b>Check of the correction:</b> after re-weighting the 2025 data to the official mix, the mean corrected chance per main category is on average 6 points from the official rate, mostly below it. The official rates are all-time averages, so the correction is rough.'])

h1('9. Explanation, advice and verdict')
h2('9.1 SHAP (beyond class)')
p('SHAP splits each campaign\'s score into one piece per input: how much that input pushed the score up or down compared with an average campaign. The pieces add up to the model\'s score (largest gap found: 0.00003). Share of the explanation by area on 2025:')
tb([['Product type and market','Founder','Ambition','Presentation','Timing','Pitch'],['34%','23%','19%','12%','9%','4%']],[40,24,26,30,24,26])
p('The four areas a founder can still change (ambition, presentation, timing, pitch) carry <b>43%</b>; what is made and who launches it carry <b>57%</b>.')
h2('9.2 Area scores (0–100)')
p('For each area the SHAP pieces of its inputs are added. The score is the share of reference campaigns (the 43,904 training campaigns) with a lower sum; 50 is typical. Bands: 0–25 weak, 26–60 to improve, 61–100 strong. Check on 2025, funded in our data by band (weak / to improve / strong): Ambition 38 / 75 / 86%, Timing 46 / 77 / 80%, Pitch 65 / 66 / 76%, Founder 41 / 62 / 94%, Presentation 55 / 68 / 82%, Product type 29 / 69 / 92%. A first campaign is shown as "no track record yet", without a score.')
h2('9.3 What to change')
p('The app tries one change at a time and recomputes the chance: lower goal (−10% to −50%, never below the stated minimum budget), campaign length (15 / 21 / 30 / 45 days), launch day (next six days), moving off Christmas, adding a video, opening a pre-launch page, 14 or 30 more days of preparation, a title of about 7 words, a full blurb. It shows the best option per lever, top 3, only if it adds at least 2 points to the middle estimate (since 25.11; before, gains were measured at the upper value, so the card could show "+19 points" next to a 23-point change of the middle estimate). On 4,000 of the 26,533 campaigns of 2025, with the minimum budget assumed at half the goal:')
b(['35% have no change worth 2 points. 43% have one worth 5 points or more; 24% one worth 10 or more.','Median gain when offered (a difference in the model\'s middle estimate, not a measured effect): pre-launch page 10.8, preparation 6.7, goal 4.6, video 4.2, blurb 3.8, length 3.7, launch day 3.5, title 3.2 points.'])
note('<b>Association, not cause.</b> A pre-launch page and long preparation stand for real groundwork (an audience built before launch). Opening a page the day before will not add these points. The app words every suggestion as "campaigns that did this were funded more often".')
h2('9.4 The verdict rule and coaching slots')
p('Since 25.11 the verdict of a campaign and the coach\'s slots are two separate things. Before, the Coach view labelled the slot holders "Coach first" and gave the rest Adjust or Launch, so a near-hopeless campaign outside the coach\'s capacity could be labelled Launch. An outside review found this; it is fixed.')
b(['<b>Verdict, the same on every screen.</b> <b>Coach first</b> = lower value below 11.5% (computed as 11.6% on re-weighted 2025 data, fixed at 11.5% by decision). <b>Adjust</b> = otherwise, if one change adds at least 5 points to the middle estimate (tested before rounding). <b>Launch</b> = everything else.','<b>Coaching slots (Coach view).</b> The riskiest teams, ranked by the lower value, up to the coach\'s capacity (default 30%, adjustable). The slider changes who gets a slot, never a verdict.'])
p('Before the change, the rule read:')
b(['<b>Coach first</b> = the riskiest share of the cohort, ranked by the lower value of the range. The share is the coach\'s capacity (default 30%, adjustable). For a single check-up without a cohort: lower value below 11.5%, which is where the riskiest 30% begin in Kickstarter\'s official mix.','<b>Adjust</b> = not in that group, and one single change is linked to at least 5 points more.','<b>Launch</b> = everything else.'])
tb([['Coach capacity','Our data: failures reached','Our data: share of the group that fails','Official mix: failures reached','Official mix: share of the group that fails'],['20%','57%','84%','35%','98%'],['<b>30%</b>','<b>75%</b>','<b>73%</b>','<b>51%</b>','<b>95%</b>'],['40%','87%','63%','66%','92%'],['50%','93%','54%','79%','88%']],[28,34,38,34,36])
p('In the official mix 56% of campaigns fail (in our data 29%). A coach who sees 30% can therefore reach at most 30 ÷ 56 = 54% of the failures; the model reaches 51%. The "official mix" figures are the 2025 test campaigns re-weighted so that funded and failed campaigns have Kickstarter\'s official proportions. Verdict mix (4,000 campaigns of 2025, new rule): Coach first 17%, Adjust 32%, Launch 51%.')
h2('9.4b Comparison with simple rules at equal capacity')
tb([['Rule (30% capacity, 2025 re-weighted to the official mix)','Failures reached','Selected teams that failed'],['Random','30.0%','56.0%'],['Lowest official main-category rate first','37.7%','70.4%'],['Highest goal for the category first','37.3%','69.7%'],['Lowest 2023–24 subcategory rate in our data first (best category rule)','44.5%','83.0%'],['Three yes/no questions: first campaign, no pre-launch page, goal above $10,000','45.2%','84.4%'],['<b>One-page scorecard: subcategory rate + the three questions</b>','47.6%','88.8%'],['<b>Our model, ranked by the lower value</b>','<b>51.1%</b>','<b>95.4%</b>'],['Maximum possible','53.6%','100%']],[100,35,35])
p('The category rules cannot tell two teams in the same category apart. A coach\'s own rule of thumb can, so on 26.11 we added rules that look at the team: three yes/no questions, and a one-page scorecard (a logistic regression on 2023–24 launches with four inputs: the subcategory rate and the three questions). Per 100 campaigns, the model finds about <b>29</b> failing teams, the scorecard <b>27</b> and the best category rule <b>25</b>. The scorecard beats the best category rule by 3.1 points of failures reached (95% paired bootstrap 2.8 to 3.5); the model is 3.5 points above the scorecard (no interval: the scorecard runs on the slim table in the repository, the model\'s per-campaign predictions need the raw data). Honest reading: against a rule a coach could keep on paper, the model adds about two failing teams per 100, not four. The category rules were first computed by the outside review and reproduced here (script stage6_rules.py); the team rules are in stage8_simple_rules.py, which runs without the raw data. It measures who gets flagged, not how much coaching helps: a failure may be impossible to prevent, and a funded team may still benefit from coaching.')
h2('9.4c Retrospective re-test on January–June 2026')
p('To rule out that the comparison only works on the year it was built on, every setting was frozen as decided on 16.11 and the test was run once on the 12,990 launches of January–June 2026. All inputs were known before 2026: the model trained on 2023–24, calibration from 2024, Kickstarter\'s official rates of 1 January 2026, ranking by the lower value, 30% capacity and the 11.5% cut. This is a retrospective re-test, not an untouched one: notebook 03 had already reported results on 2026 H1 before 16.11 (AUC 0.866, model comparisons and a 30%-capacity figure of 81.0% in the observed sample). No rule or setting was chosen on 2026, but the files cannot prove that nothing was influenced by it.')
tb([['Rule (30% capacity, Jan–Jun 2026, official mix: 56.1% fail)','Failures reached','Selected teams that failed'],['Random','30.0%','56.1%'],['Lowest official main-category rate first','37.9%','70.8%'],['Highest goal for the category first','35.8%','67.0%'],['<b>Lowest subcategory rate first, 2023–24 data (strongest category rule)</b>','45.0%','84.2%'],['Lowest subcategory rate first, 2023–25 data','44.6%','83.4%'],['The same, only outcomes resolved before 1 January 2026','44.9%','83.9%'],['Three yes/no questions (fitted on 2023–24)','45.0%','84.1%'],['One-page scorecard (fitted on 2023–24; built on 26.11, after 2026 had been seen)','47.9%','89.6%'],['<b>Our model, frozen</b>','<b>51.1%</b>','<b>95.7%</b>'],['Maximum possible','53.5%','100%']],[100,35,35])
p('Against the strongest category rule the model reaches 6.1 points more of all failures (difference on the full sample; 95% paired bootstrap interval 5.5 to 7.0). Against the 2023–25 rule it is +6.6 (5.9 to 7.2), against the calendar-purged rule +6.3 (5.7 to 7.0). The interval resamples campaigns with the model and the re-weighting held fixed; it does not cover repeated creators, unseen failures, uncertainty in the official rates, retraining or the effect of coaching. The fixed 11.5% cut flags 27% of the re-weighted 2026 cohort, against a target of 30%, and 96% of those failed. The same calendar check on 2025 changes the strongest rule from 44.5% to 44.3%. Against the scorecard the model is 3.2 points ahead in 2026 (scorecard minus best category rule: +2.9, 95% interval 2.4 to 3.5), the same picture as in 2025: 29, 27 and 25 failing teams per 100.')
h2('9.5 Other elements')
b(['<b>Product types that do badly:</b> when the product-type score is 15 or lower (13% of 2025 campaigns; 21% funded in our data against 78% for the rest), the app tells the coach that changing the campaign set-up will not fix it.','<b>Explanation tree (class):</b> a depth-3 decision tree on readable inputs, shown in the Check-up under "Why this result?".','<b>Similar campaigns (beyond class):</b> the 10 nearest past campaigns in the same main category. Their funded share alone predicts 2025 outcomes with AUC within category 0.791.','<b>Tested and cut:</b> k-means archetypes (silhouette 0.13–0.17, too weak); a regression for money raised (R² 0.675, not used in the app).'])

h1('10. The app')
tb([['Page','What it does'],['Home','The idea, the three verdicts, the data in numbers, the official-mix business claim'],['Check-up','Form in four steps (product, money and timing, preparation, track record) → verdict, a horizontal bar with the chance range (lower value, middle estimate, upper value), best next step, four bars for the areas a founder can change, context for product type and founder'],['Goal optimizer','How the chance changes with the goal; recommended goal above the minimum budget; explains when the advice is "keep your goal"'],['Coach view','Upload a CSV with all teams (template provided); one ranked list from riskiest to safest; a line marks where the coach\'s capacity ends (coaching slots above it); each row keeps its own verdict, range and top change; capacity slider; download'],['Similar campaigns','The 10 most similar past campaigns with links (no creator names or ids; source Web Robots credited)'],['Benchmarks','Kickstarter\'s official funding rates by main category, with chart and table'],['How it works','Method, calibration chart, counts, limits, in plain words']],[36,134])
h2('10.1 How it is built')
b(['<b>Files:</b> <font name="BB">app.py</font> (pages), <font name="BB">ui.py</font> (design: styles, range bar, area bars, badges), <font name="BB">preprocess.py</font> (loads the model files once with caching and turns the form or an uploaded row into the 32 inputs, in the training column order), <font name="BB">features.py</font>, <font name="BB">modeling.py</font>, <font name="BB">advice.py</font> (class Advisor: chances, SHAP, area scores, fixes, verdict).','<b>Model files</b> (about 5 MB): the XGBoost model, the text model, a file with all constants (calibration parameters, official rates, reference values for the area scores, category medians, test metrics, benchmarks), and a slim table of 83,427 past campaigns for the similar-campaigns page. <b>No raw data is in the repository.</b>','<b>Versions pinned</b> in requirements.txt: Python 3.13, streamlit 1.67.0, xgboost 3.4.1, scikit-learn 1.9.1, pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.3, altair 6.3.0, joblib 1.6.0, vaderSentiment 3.3.2, pyarrow 26.0.1.','<b>Deployment:</b> GitHub repository → Streamlit Community Cloud. Every push to the main branch redeploys the app.'])
h2('10.2 Redesign')
p('After the first working version, the whole interface was redesigned (brand, landing page, two-column check-up with gauge, triage board, restyled charts, mobile layout). Rule: presentation only; no model and no number changed. Safeguards: the old version is kept on the branch <font name="BB">backup-v1.0</font>; the redesign was built on a separate branch and merged only after Fedor approved it.')
h2('10.3 Second round: outside review and Decision Desk (25.11)')
p('An outside review by ChatGPT (GPT-6.2) graded the project at 5.25 / 6 and named three main weaknesses: the coach claim sounded more certain than the evidence; "most likely" gave the middle of the range too much authority; and the founder-control statement sounded causal. ChatGPT then drafted a new layout ("Decision Desk"). Claude tested it on a separate branch (all pages run; every number identical to the version before) and merged it after Fedor approved. Changes: the gauge became a horizontal range bar; the three Coach view columns became one ranked list with a capacity line; Home leads with "Open Coach view" and "Check a campaign"; "most likely" became "middle estimate"; the 95% claim is always stated as the result of a re-weighted test on 2025 campaigns; the founder-control title refers to the model\'s explanation. After the update the live server kept the old design file in memory; one line in app.py now reloads it.')
h2('10.4 Third round: examiner-style review (25.11)')
p('A second outside review (ChatGPT, GPT-6.2 Astra, in a fresh chat with all documents) again graded the project 5.25 / 6 and checked our files in detail. Claude confirmed three errors in the code and fixed them the same day: (1) the verdict and the coaching slots are now separate (section 9.4); (2) gains are measured on the middle estimate, the number shown, so "+23 points" now matches "40% → 63%"; (3) the two ends of the range are named by source, because in Dance the estimate adjusted to official rates is the higher one. Smaller corrections: a gain of 4.6 points is no longer rounded up to pass the 5-point test; 163 (not 174) categories count in the within-category AUC; several document details. The deck was restructured: the Coach view comes right after the decision, slides 8 and 9 are merged, the six input areas and the full model comparison moved to backup, and slide 10 now shows the comparison with simple rules (section 9.4b). The example campaign has a fixed launch date (15 January 2027), so it gives the same result every day.')
h2('10.5 Fourth round: independent examiner review (26.11)')
p('A review in a separate session (Claude, model Fable 5.2, for Nico) graded the project 5.0 / 6 as it stood and 5.5 with fixes, and tested the live app. What it found and what we did:')
b(['<b>Comparator too weak.</b> Slide 10 compared the model only with rules that look at the category. A one-page scorecard with three questions about the team finds 27 failing teams per 100, against 29 for the model and 25 for the best category rule. Slide 10, the script and the docs now say 29 / 27 / 25 (section 9.4b).','<b>Round-number goals.</b> Since 25.11 the app recomputed the round-number flag for every goal it tested, so a suggested goal could win only because it was a round number ($9,000: +5.3 points; $8,999: +1.8), and the Goal optimizer curve had bumps. Now the flag keeps the plan\'s own value for goal suggestions and for the curve. Our own check found that holding the flag everywhere would let Check-up flag a recommended goal as riskiest (101 of 213 recommendations for round-goal plans in the riskiest group, in 3,000 plans built from 2025 campaigns), so the recommended goal and the three-goal table are scored exactly, as in Check-up. The figures of section 9.3 were computed on 25.11 before the per-goal flag was introduced; re-run on 26.11 with the corrected code they come out the same (with the per-goal flag: 35.1% instead of 34.5% without a 2-point change, 14 of 4,000 verdicts different).','<b>Rounding on Launch cards.</b> Launch means that no change adds 5 points, but rounding two percentages could show "+5" (in a test on 1,500 plans built from 2025 campaigns, 47 Launch cards). The card now shows the unrounded gain in that case, and the Coach view export has a column "Gain before rounding".','<b>Wording.</b> Two script sentences that contradicted our own limits were removed ("a concrete next step: a pre-launch page", "we test once on 2025"); the 2025 test year is described as not untouched (section 7.1); Q&amp;A question 17 added; semantic layer v1.6 (approved by Fedor on 26.11).'])
p('The example campaign and the example cohort give the same results as before on every page, including the Goal optimizer table.')
h2('10.6 Testing')
b(['Automated tests with Streamlit\'s test tool: every page runs without error, on three example campaigns.','Screenshots at desktop and phone widths to check the layout.','On the live site after the merge: all 7 pages load without errors and show the expected values (checked again on 25.11; the check-up example, with its launch date fixed to 15 January 2027 so it gives the same result every day: Adjust, middle estimate 40%, between 23% and 60%).'])

h1('11. Verification: what was checked, and how')
tb([['Check','Result'],['Our counts against Kickstarter\'s official statistics','Found the selection bias (84.5% / 41.6%)'],['Suspiciously high AUC investigated','Cause: category-level selection through the listing cap, not a leaking input → headline metric moved to within category'],['History inputs recomputed with separate code on 695 rows (check_history.py; 200 rows in notebook 02)','0 mismatches; a planted error (counting campaigns that had not yet ended) is caught in every row'],['Leak through missing values','Shown and removed (section 5.1)'],['Title edits during campaigns','Rare (3.2% / 1.5%)'],['SHAP pieces add up to the score','Largest gap 0.00003'],['Calibration','Largest gap 4.7 points after, 9.5 before'],['Middle of the range against a public figure','78% vs official 80% (one benchmark)'],['Notebooks 01–04','02–04 re-run from start to end on 16.11. On 26.11 on the raw data: notebook 01 run with outputs against the official totals of 1 January 2026 (84.5% funded, 41.6% failed only, 47.4% failed, cancelled or suspended); notebook 04 re-run with the current code, output matches stage5_gain.py and the result files'],['Comparison with team rules (stage8_simple_rules.py)','Written in a separate review session; re-run here with identical output'],['Goal optimizer against Check-up','Every recommended goal stays out of the riskiest group when entered in Check-up (by construction; tested on 3,000 plans)'],['All five charts of the deck','Numbers recomputed with separate code from the saved test predictions; all match (the SHAP shares were traced to the notebook output, not recomputed a second time)']],[75,95])
note('<b>Limit of these checks.</b> Most were done by the same assistant that wrote the code; the chart check in particular was a self-check. The reviews of 25.11 (ChatGPT) and 26.11 (a separate Claude session) were independent of the code-writing session.')

